# Docker deployment environment (production Compose).
# Create .env.production and fill the required secrets:
#   node scripts/init-production-env.mjs   # creates .env.production + fills secrets
# Then run the stack explicitly against it:
#   docker compose --env-file .env.production -f docker-compose.yml up -d
# (Passing --env-file replaces the default .env as Compose's interpolation
# file — the dev .env is never read for deployment, though host shell
# environment variables can still override individual values. Conversely, no
# dev tooling (pnpm dev / vite / drizzle / vitest) ever reads this file.)
# Source-build verification (contributors / PR acceptance) builds THIS
# checkout explicitly (BOTH images) and runs the same file against the local
# tags (#626). When the checkout is also a dev checkout, EXAM_DATA_ROOT must
# point OUTSIDE it (#631): the default ./data would let postgres create
# root-owned runtime state inside the source tree and break
# `pnpm format:check`:
#   docker build --target runner -t exam-local:dev .
#   docker build --target web-runner -t exam-local:web-dev .
#   EXAM_IMAGE=exam-local:dev EXAM_WEB_IMAGE=exam-local:web-dev \
#     EXAM_DATA_ROOT="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/exam-prod-rehearsal" \
#     docker compose --env-file .env.production -f docker-compose.yml up -d

# ── Required secrets (no defaults; init-production-env fills, never rotates)
JWT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Topology ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=exam
POSTGRES_DB=exam

# Docker bridge subnet for the bundled stack (networks.exam-net). The
# bundled TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS default derives from the same value, so
# overriding this moves both together. Change it ONLY on collision with
# the host's LAN, VPN, or an existing Docker network: a Docker-network
# overlap fails at `up` with "Pool overlaps...", but a LAN/VPN overlap
# can silently break container networking — pick a free RFC1918 subnet.
# EXAM_DOCKER_SUBNET=172.28.0.0/24

# Prebuilt production images, pinned to the release version. init-production-env
# derives both from .release-version (ghcr.io/jnhu76/exam and
# ghcr.io/jnhu76/exam-web, vX.Y.Z); an explicit value always wins. Offline
# installs: pull on a connected machine, `docker save`, transfer,
# `docker load`, keep these same references.
EXAM_IMAGE=
EXAM_WEB_IMAGE=

# Public HTTP ingress port published by the `web` service — one nginx that
# is both the static SPA server and the sole public production edge (#585;
# the API on 3000 stays internal). Anything other than the default 80 (or
# any non-localhost address) must set CORS_ORIGIN / PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN to
# the address users actually browse.
#
# Transport policy: the PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN scheme decides HTTPS-specific
# behavior platform-wide. `http://...` (LAN HTTP — supported on an isolated
# or appropriately trusted LAN) ships auth cookies WITHOUT Secure and no
# HSTS; `https://...` keeps Secure cookies, HSTS, and CSP
# upgrade-insecure-requests. APP_MODE is always `production` for both —
# TLS is an ingress property, never an application mode.
# EXAM_PORT=80
# CORS_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.5
# PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.5

# APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Shanghai
# TZ=Asia/Shanghai

# ── Trusted reverse proxy (bundled web nginx edge, #585) ─────────────────
# CIDRs whose sockets may rewrite the client IP via X-Forwarded-For
# (comma-separated; a bare IP means that one host). The bundled topology
# needs NO value here: docker-compose.yml derives the default from
# EXAM_DOCKER_SUBNET (default 172.28.0.0/24 — networks.exam-net), the same
# topology fact that names the bridge subnet, so request.ip is the real
# LAN client out of the box. Unset/empty would collapse all candidates
# onto the edge's bridge IP (one rate-limit/audit identity) — only
# meaningful for a direct-client topology without the bundled edge.
# PRECONDITION: never cover the candidate client network (and never a
# client LAN CIDR): a trusted CIDR that covers clients lets them forge
# their rate-limit/audit identity. The bundled default respects this — it
# names the stack's own bridge, whose peers are all stack infrastructure.
# Trust-all CIDRs
# (0.0.0.0/0, ::/0) are rejected at startup. The bundled edge
# (deploy/nginx/web.conf) is directly client-facing and REPLACES
# X-Forwarded-For with the real client address ($remote_addr — appending
# would let clients forge the header inside the trusted walk).
# Override ONLY when candidates reach the API through an EXTERNAL proxy
# instead of the bundled edge — name that proxy's addresses:
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=192.0.2.10/32
# See the deployment runbook §2 "Rate-limit identity, trusted proxies, and
# sizing" for external-proxy and shared-NAT cases.

# ── Optional: Redis (enable with `--profile redis`) ──────────────────────
# REDIS_PASSWORD=<secret>
# REDIS_URL=redis://:<secret>@redis:6379

# ── Optional: email (defaults: disabled, fake transport — nothing sent) ──
# EMAIL_ENABLED=true
# EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.your-org.internal
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=

# ── Optional: first-install launchpad token ──────────────────────────────
# LAUNCHPAD_SETUP_TOKEN=
